Problem

The existing Officer Clif interface asks open-ended questions with no prompting for case-specific details, resulting in submissions that frequently lacked the information investigation officers needed.
Business/operational problem
Officers spent significant time chasing missing details that could have been captured upfront, reducing their capacity for higher-priority work. Complainant reports may contain either too much or too little information, requiring officers to spend time reading through reports to gather crucial information needed for cases.
User problem
Citizens lodging reports online often are not aware of the case specific information police officers require for investigation. This results in a lot of back and forth between the complainant and the police officer, resulting in an lenghtened intial information gathering session for the complainant.
Research & Discovery
Understanding the As-Is System
Before designing the AI chatbot, I conducted a heuristic analysis of the existing report lodging experience currently deployed at Self-Help Kiosks (SHKs) across Singapore's Electronic Police Centres.

The existing system already used a conversational metaphor: a 3D animated avatar named "Officer Clif" guided users through the process, asking questions in natural language. The flow covered six sections, supported Singpass authentication with MyInfo auto-population, and allowed users to save a draft and return within 7 days.

While the approach was well-intentioned, walking through the full flow surfaced several pain points:
1
The chatbot was a form in disguise.
OBSERVATION
Officer Clif asked questions, but the system didn't interpret or adapt to responses. Each section was a structured form regardless of the case details the complainant lodged. This also meant the system had no way to detect missing or incomplete information; it would accept whatever was submitted and move on.
CONSEQUENCE
The information gathered is preliminary and generic, requiring follow up for the police for more case specific information.
2
The free-text section gave citizens no guidance.
OBSERVATION
The “What happened?” section presented a blank text field with no indication of what level of detail was expected, what case-type-specific information was needed, or how officers would use the report.
CONSEQUENCE
Complainants are not aware of relevant information that may help officers in their investigations, and thus do not provide them when lodging a report.
3
The sentence-completion format created cognitive friction.
OBSERVATION
Filling in details mid-sentence felt unnatural, particularly for users with lower digital or language literacy.
CONSEQUENCE
The format required citizens to adapt to the system's grammar rather than describe events in their own words.
4
Error states were easy to miss.
OBSERVATION
When Singpass MyInfo retrieval failed due to an NRIC mismatch, the error appeared only as a dismissible banner at the top of the form.
CONSEQUENCE
Users who miss error notifications will face frustration during an experience which requires clarity and comfort.
As-Is User Journey
Mapping the end-to-end flow revealed a six-stage journey from kiosk landing to submission:
1
Landing & Avatar Introduction
Officer Clif greeting and service overview.

2
Identity Verification
Singpass QR scan, NRIC, or passport validation.

3
Personal Details
MyInfo auto-population or manual entry (Section 1 of 6).

4
Incident Details
Sections 2–4: What, when, where, and who involved.

5
Supporting Information
Section 5: Witnesses, CCTV footage, and items.

6
Review & Submit
Section 6: Final summary and official declaration.
The critical gap was concentrated in Section 2: despite the structured flow, the free-text "What happened?" field consistently produced reports that were either too vague or missing case type-specific details that investigation officers (IOs) needed to assess and process the case. The resulting back-and-forth between IOs and complainants was the core operational inefficiency the product was built to solve.
Design
Structuring the conversation flow
The conversation flow structure was defined by the product manager in collaboration with SPF stakeholders, drawing on existing investigation question frameworks. My role was to translate this flow into an interaction system that complainants could interact with easily. The flow map below shows the structure I was designing within.

I identified different input types the questions types would require, and designed the look and feel of each input type. Due to time constraints, component designs were adapted from an existing UI Kit.

Checkboxes for questions allowing multiple selections
Radio buttons for mutually exclusive options
Free-text input for open-ended incident descriptions
Structured form fields for precise data like dates, times, and item values
Progress indicator — navigating security constraints

Vartion 1: Flat structure — all steps at same level

Variation 2: Grouped structure — sub-steps nested under parent sections

Chosen Variation: Simplified structure — condensed to 5 top-level steps
I proposed a step-progress sidebar as a core navigation element, giving citizens visibility into how far along they were in the process. This was met with resistance from the engineering team, who raised a concern that exposing the question structure could allow malicious actors to predict and game the AI's logic. I worked with the team to find a middle ground: the progress indicator would show stage labels (Before You Begin, Incident Overview, Incident Details, Confirm Report, Submit Report) without surfacing the specific questions within each stage. This preserved user orientation without compromising the system's security posture.
Loading state and avatar design
I also designed a loading animation and avatar character for the chatbot — a friendly robot in a police uniform — to give the AI a visual identity that felt approachable without impersonating an actual officer. This was proposed to be used during loading states while the system would be processing user input, to provide some warmth to the journey of loding a police report. Although initial discussions with stakeholders shaped the final design of the avatar, they were eventually not used. This shaped the look and feel of the report lodging process to be more formal, though retaining its friendliness through other means such as copywriting.
Usability Testing
Participants
Testing was conducted across two rounds (7 and 9 October 2025) with 9 participants from a cross-section of Singapore public sector professionals with varying levels of digital literacy. Participants were walked through multiple report lodging scenarios (physical assault, scam, theft) to test different chatbot conversation flows.

‍Satisfaction ratings were collected across four dimensions on a 1–6 scale: Navigation, Ease of Use, Content & Language, and Overall Satisfaction.
Key Tasks Tested
Acknowledging terms and navigating the onboarding flow
Entering incident details (date, time, location) via the chatbot
Interacting with the AI chatbot to describe the incident in free text
Editing previously submitted details
Previewing and confirming the AI-generated report summary
Submitting the report

Top Findings & Design Changes
1
Contradictory messaging around editing caused user confusion
OBSERVATION
The system edit affordance (a small pen icon) was not obvious. Multiple participants were confused or missed the option entirely. Average scores for the editing step dropped to 4.9 across all dimensions, the lowest in the test.
WHAT CHANGED
Improved visibility of the edit affordance by replacing with it with a text button with the label 'Edit' instead.
2
The AI chatbot asked redundant or mismatched follow-up questions
OBSERVATION
When participants typed a detailed free-text description upfront, the chatbot still prompted them for information already provided (e.g. asking again about medical attention after the participant had already mentioned a clinic). In one case, a "yes" answer about CCTVs triggered a follow-up asking for a person's contact details — a logic mismatch. This eroded trust in the AI's capability.
CONSEQUENCE
Flagged as an engineering issue for the AI model to cross-reference existing inputs before prompting follow-up questions.
3
The "Confirm Report" page created anxiety rather than confidence
OBSERVATION
The final review page was labelled "Confirm Report" with only an "Edit Summary" button — leading participants to worry that clicking edit would cause them to lose the AI-generated summary. Several users couldn't find a way to confirm without editing.
CONSEQUENCE
Split up editing and previewing reports funcionality by allowing users to enter edit mode by clicking a button labelled "Edit" for each individual report section. Also updated the primary CTA button label to "Preview Report".
What went well
Positive feedback was consistent across the higher-scoring steps. Participants praised the conversational chatbot format for keeping them on track, the interactive map for location input (searchable by postal code and location name), and the ability to proofread and edit the AI-generated summary before submission. The Singpass auto-population of personal details was consistently noted as a time-saver.
Final Design
Through further iterations and monitoring of feedback, I developed the designs while maintaing these principles throughout:
Progressive disclosure through guided conversation
Rather than presenting all information at once, R-COP uses progressive disclosure to ensure that the support material that guides users to lodge their reports are not overwhelming, and is available when they need it. For instance, additional information are provided and hidden in dropdowns, and important key sentences are bolded to ensure greater clarity and hierachy in the information presented. The conversation flow is also able to detect the case type the user wishes to report, and asks relevant questions to the case at hand.

Natural language over procedural language
Police report forms traditionally use formal, procedural language that can feel intimidating or ambiguous to the general public. R-COP uses plain, conversational phrasing throughout. For example, rather than "State the date and time of the incident," the chatbot asks "When did the incident happen?" — with a supporting note in regular weight that explicitly gives citizens permission to estimate if they don't recall exactly.

Clarity and control over AI generated portions
While observing user feedback post-launched, we noticed that some complainants were not satisfied with the AI generated reports, due to over-summarisation, or missing out of aspects of their reports they deemed important. We decided to allow users to be able to refer back to their original initial input they submitted to the chatbot, so they could gain more control over the final report they submitted.
