Final Design

Outcome
During usability testing, participants completed the report lodging flow in approximately 4 minutes on average across the two rounds. For context, the existing system was estimated to take approximately 15 minutes for a comparable submission. The difference in completion time reflects the core design intent: replacing a six-section form with a guided conversational flow that structures the process for the user. A formal before/after time comparison would require controlled testing on both systems with the same participants and scenarios.

After rollout to 9 kiosks across 7 divisional HQ Police Centres, users consistently gave ratings above 4 out of 5 for overall satisfaction\*.

On the operational side, feedback indicated that the AI's case classification accuracy required further refinement — likely a result of training data gaps and the need to more precisely define classification categories through additional research sessions with domain experts. This highlighted a key learning: for AI-assisted government services, the design and research process doesn't end at launch. Ongoing iteration between designers, product managers, and subject matter experts is essential to ensure the AI performs reliably across the full range of real-world scenarios.

\*R-COP was launched on 9 kiosks across 7 divisional HQ Electronic Police Centres in Oct 2025, with a roadmap to expand to 50% of all kiosks in Singapore by April 2026 and full nationwide rollout by end of 2026.
Reflections
Designing an AI-assisted product for a public-facing government context taught me things that a typical SaaS project wouldn't.
Designing for AI failure modes, not just ideal flows.
In most UX work, edge cases are about missing data or user error. With AI, the failure modes are different — hallucinations, misclassified inputs, redundant follow-up questions, responses that don't match what the user just said. Designing for these required building explicit trust scaffolding into the UI: the yellow highlight system for AI-generated content that needs verification, the side-by-side edit modal so users could always see their original words, and the "Restore Generated Content" safety net.
Making AI output feel transparent, not opaque.
Citizens submitting a police report need to feel confident that what gets submitted is accurate and theirs. The challenge was designing an AI that genuinely helps without making users feel like they've lost authorship of their own report. The visual distinction between the AI-generated summary and editable content, combined with the explicit declaration checkbox, was how we resolved that tension.
Security and compliance shape design decisions.
Working closely with engineers on a government product meant that design choices, such as how long data persists in a session, what gets logged, how edit states are handled had security and legal implications I hadn't encountered were raised. Learning to raise these questions early, and to arrive at solutions collaboratively rather than handing off a spec and hoping for the best, made me a more effective design partner on a cross-functional team.
What I'd do differently.
The case classification issue that surfaced post-launch suggests that earlier research sessions with SPF officers to define classification taxonomy — before training data was finalised — could have reduced the need for rework. In hindsight, I'd advocate for those sessions as a design research deliverable, not just an engineering input.
